    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm working on some dark chocolate batches and I keep running into an issue with fat bloom. The surface gets this dull, white streaky look after it sits for a bit. Can you explain what's actually happening chemically and how I can fix my tempering process to stop it?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Fat bloom occurs when cocoa butter migrates to the chocolate's surface due to improper tempering, causing the fat crystals to destabilize and re-solidify into larger, light crystals. To fix this, you must strictly temper your chocolate to ensure a stable mass of fine crystal formation at roughly 30–32°C (86–90°F), allowing them to remain locked in that state. Regularly practice snapping tests on your finished batches to verify the glossy, snap-free finish you aim for.",
