    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks for that. So it's basically a crystallization issue. I see what you mean about the stability of the crystals. Is there any specific way temperature fluctuations during storage trigger this \"bloom\" effect more aggressively? Like, does a sudden change in the environment make the cocoa butter move to the surface faster?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, sharp temperature fluctuations act as a destabilizing force that melts the stable cocoa butter crystals in the short range, allowing them to spread and recrystallize loosely on the surface when they re-solidify. While cold storage often accelerates this by companding the mix, it is the air temperature that melts it; if the rectified temperature of chocolate rises above 32°C (90°F), the crystal structure weakens, allowing unstable fat to seep out. To prevent this, maintain a constant, cool environment around 15–18°C (60–65°F) with no drafts near your finished bars.",
      "source": "target",
