    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. It sounds like even a small environmental change can ruin the whole batch. When you mention the \"bloom\" appearing on the surface, is there any way to tell if it's just fat bloom or if the actual cocoa solids are starting to separate or degrade too? I just want to be sure I'm not wasting a whole batch of high-quality couverture.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Fat bloom appears as a whitish layer where the fat has reached the surface, whereas sugar bloom shows a dusty, white texture hundreds of spots where moisture has dissolved and recrystallized sugars. If the chocolate inside is still crackling when snapped and has a tight, shiny texture beneath the bloom, the fats are likely just out of place and the batch is still edible. To distinguish these, inspect the flavor and texture; off flavors indicate sugar bloom or mold, while a return to normal taste with the white look suggests only a cosmetic fat bloom issue.",
